# Line-ending policy for files synced from the MiSTer template (../Template_MiSTer).
#
# Scoped to EXACT paths on purpose: the repo currently has a mix of committed
# line endings in sys/ (LF, CRLF, and a few mixed). A broad pattern like *.v or
# *.sv would force-renormalize those existing blobs and produce huge whole-file
# diffs. These exact paths cover only the files involved in the template update,
# all of which are already stored as LF, so pinning them to eol=lf is churn-free.
#
# Effect: these files are treated as text and always stored/checked out with LF,
# regardless of the editor or OS (immune to core.autocrlf).
*.sv          text eol=lf

# Quartus project files. Quartus on the Windows build box rewrites these with
# CRLF on every compile, which otherwise shows up as a whole-file diff. Pinning
# to eol=lf makes git normalize the working tree back to LF, so the Windows
# rewrites become invisible. .qsf/.sdc blobs are already LF (churn-free); .qpf
# was committed with CRLF and is renormalized to LF in this same commit.
*.qsf         text eol=lf
*.qpf         text eol=lf
*.sdc         text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. Nothing pinned them before, so on a Windows checkout
# core.autocrlf gave them CRLF — and a CRLF `#!/bin/sh` script dies the moment it
# runs anywhere POSIX. Hit for real on 2026-08-29: docs/tools/make-fastboot-rom.sh
# under WSL failed with `set: Illegal option -` (dash reading `set -e\r`), which
# blocked building the sim's fast-boot ROM. These scripts run on Linux, macOS, WSL
# and on the MiSTer itself, so LF is not cosmetic here.
*.sh          text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binaries: never convert, never diff as text.
#
# This is the safety net that matters most for moving the repo between machines.
# Disk images, ROMs and the prebuilt .tgz bundles all contain stray CR bytes, so
# a heuristic can misread them as text -- and a single CRLF conversion silently
# corrupts a 2 GB image or a boot ROM in a way nothing downstream would notice
# until the machine refuses to boot. Marking them explicitly means no present or
# future text rule can reach them.
*.hda         binary
*.dsk         binary
*.adf         binary
*.rom         binary
*.nvr         binary
*.BIN         binary
*.tgz         binary
*.gz          binary
*.zip         binary
*.pdf         binary
*.png         binary
*.jpg         binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Executed or parsed by POSIX tools, where CRLF is a hard failure rather than
# cosmetic: a CRLF shebang makes the kernel hunt for an interpreter with a
# trailing CR, and a CRLF Makefile feeds \r into every recipe line. (*.sh is
# pinned above, after exactly that bit us under WSL.) All of these blobs are
# already stored LF, so pinning them is churn-free.
*.py          text eol=lf
*.tcl         text eol=lf
Makefile      text eol=lf
*.mk          text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.asm         text eol=lf

# Windows batch files want CRLF. They are already stored that way, so pin them
# byte-exact rather than switching to store-LF/check-out-CRLF: that would be a
# whole-file rewrite for no behavioural gain.
*.bat         -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT normalized, deliberately:
#
#   sys/**                  vendored from ../Template_MiSTer with a mix of LF
#                           and CRLF blobs. Renormalizing would turn every
#                           future template sync into a whole-file diff and
#                           destroy the ability to diff against upstream. The
#                           files are Verilog/Tcl read by Quartus and Verilator,
#                           both of which accept CRLF, so there is nothing to
#                           gain. (*.sv above predates this and stays as-is.)
#   SingleStepTests/gen/*.c/.h   generated corpora, megabytes each; the diff
#                           would swamp history for no benefit.
#   verilator/sim/m68k_dasm.*    vendored disassembler, same reasoning as sys/.
#
# Enforced with `-text` (check out byte-exact as committed) rather than left
# unmarked: an unmarked file still gets rewritten by `git add --renormalize` or
# by core.autocrlf on a Windows checkout, which is how a "targeted" policy
# quietly turns into a 5,000-line diff. `-text` is what actually holds the line.
#
# For files under sys/ this supersedes the *.sv pin at the top of this file.
# No behaviour changes today -- those blobs are stored LF, and `-text` checks
# them out as stored -- it only stops them being renormalized later.
sys/**                              -text
verilator/sim/m68k_dasm.h           -text
verilator/sim/m68k_dasm_stubs.cpp   -text
SingleStepTests/gen/*.c             -text
SingleStepTests/gen/*.h             -text
rtl/mycore.v                        -text
rtl/lfsr.v                          -text
